<p:sld xmlns:a="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/drawingml/2006/main" xmlns:ns2="http://schemas.microsoft.com/office/drawing/2014/main" xmlns:ns3="http://schemas.microsoft.com/office/powerpoint/2010/main" xmlns:p="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/presentationml/2006/main"><p:cSld><p:spTree><p:nvGrpSpPr><p:cNvPr id="1" name="" /><p:cNvGrpSpPr /><p:nvPr /></p:nvGrpSpPr><p:grpSpPr><a:xfrm><a:off x="0" y="0" /><a:ext cx="0" cy="0" /><a:chOff x="0" y="0" /><a:chExt cx="0" cy="0" /></a:xfrm></p:grpSpPr><p:sp><p:nvSpPr><p:cNvPr id="2" name="Title 1"><a:extLst><a:ext uri="{FF2B5EF4-FFF2-40B4-BE49-F238E27FC236}"><ns2:creationId id="{37945862-97FF-6F80-002E-77AC6E4B044D}" /></a:ext></a:extLst></p:cNvPr><p:cNvSpPr><a:spLocks noGrp="1" /></p:cNvSpPr><p:nvPr><p:ph type="title" /></p:nvPr></p:nvSpPr><p:spPr /><p:txBody><a:bodyPr /><a:lstStyle /><a:p><a:r><a:rPr lang="en-US" /><a:t>Interpretation, Analysis, and Discussion</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr lang="en-US" /></a:p></p:txBody></p:sp><p:sp><p:nvSpPr><p:cNvPr id="3" name="Content Placeholder 2"><a:extLst><a:ext uri="{FF2B5EF4-FFF2-40B4-BE49-F238E27FC236}"><ns2:creationId id="{DDA26F5F-AACE-2314-6029-00A01FBB5FF6}" /></a:ext></a:extLst></p:cNvPr><p:cNvSpPr><a:spLocks noGrp="1" /></p:cNvSpPr><p:nvPr><p:ph sz="half" idx="1" /></p:nvPr></p:nvSpPr><p:spPr /><p:txBody><a:bodyPr><a:normAutofit fontScale="92500" lnSpcReduction="10000" /></a:bodyPr><a:lstStyle /><a:p><a:r><a:rPr lang="en-US" /><a:t>? Validation accuracy is nearly identical across all four modes, so the main differences are systems-level rather than model-level.</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr lang="en-US" /></a:p><a:p><a:r><a:rPr lang="en-US" /><a:t>? Epoch checkpointing is the cheapest during normal training.</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr lang="en-US" /></a:p><a:p><a:r><a:rPr lang="en-US" /><a:t>? Convergence-aware checkpointing adds intelligent scheduling, but in current runs it does not reduce checkpoint count.</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr lang="en-US" /></a:p><a:p><a:r><a:rPr lang="en-US" /><a:t>? Hash ring increases checkpointing overhead during training, but it enables recovery from local cache instead of central storage.</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr lang="en-US" /></a:p></p:txBody></p:sp><p:sp><p:nvSpPr><p:cNvPr id="4" name="Content Placeholder 3"><a:extLst><a:ext uri="{FF2B5EF4-FFF2-40B4-BE49-F238E27FC236}"><ns2:creationId id="{2D57852F-0AD6-61F7-34ED-0A7DB09A547D}" /></a:ext></a:extLst></p:cNvPr><p:cNvSpPr><a:spLocks noGrp="1" /></p:cNvSpPr><p:nvPr><p:ph sz="half" idx="2" /></p:nvPr></p:nvSpPr><p:spPr /><p:txBody><a:bodyPr><a:normAutofit fontScale="92500" lnSpcReduction="10000" /></a:bodyPr><a:lstStyle /><a:p><a:r><a:rPr lang="en-US" /><a:t>? The most promising result is convergence + hash ring for recovery time.</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr lang="en-US" /></a:p><a:p><a:r><a:rPr lang="en-US" /><a:t>? Current evidence suggests a tradeoff: higher checkpoint overhead in exchange for potentially better fault recovery behavior.</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr lang="en-US" /></a:p><a:p><a:r><a:rPr lang="en-US" /><a:t>? Therefore, the contribution is best presented as an implemented comparative study of checkpointing tradeoffs with a promising recovery-oriented direction.</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr lang="en-US" /></a:p></p:txBody></p:sp></p:spTree><p:extLst><p:ext uri="{BB962C8B-B14F-4D97-AF65-F5344CB8AC3E}"><ns3:creationId val="2292282507" /></p:ext></p:extLst></p:cSld><p:clrMapOvr><a:masterClrMapping /></p:clrMapOvr></p:sld>